WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.560
Choose a safe, clear, obstacle-free place where walking slowly will not put you or anyone else at risk.

2
00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:14.290
Do not use this practice near traffic, stairs, water, or anywhere that requires your full external attention.

3
00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:20.850
You may stop, open your eyes more fully, or end the practice whenever needed.

4
00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.560
Stand in a way that feels steady.

5
00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:37.080
Let your eyes remain open with a soft, useful gaze.

6
00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:43.610
Notice the contact of both feet with the ground and the ordinary work of balancing.

7
00:00:57.627 --> 00:01:01.227
Before moving, feel one breath arrive and leave.

8
00:01:02.927 --> 00:01:05.457
There is no need to coordinate breathing with steps.

9
00:01:07.157 --> 00:01:10.657
Let the breath keep its own rhythm while the body prepares to walk.

10
00:01:21.169 --> 00:01:22.979
Begin at a comfortable pace.

11
00:01:24.679 --> 00:01:28.159
It can be slower than usual, but it does not need to look unusual.

12
00:01:29.859 --> 00:01:32.489
Feel the first shift of weight from one foot toward the other.

13
00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:51.630
Notice one foot becoming lighter.

14
00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:56.290
The heel lifts, then the rest of the foot follows.

15
00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:00.950
The leg moves through space, and the foot meets the ground again.

16
00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:05.380
You do not need to name every part while it happens.

17
00:02:26.405 --> 00:02:27.635
Let the other step unfold.

18
00:02:29.335 --> 00:02:33.485
Weight transfers, one foot releases, travels, and returns.

19
00:02:35.185 --> 00:02:37.325
Walking already knows how to organize itself.

20
00:02:39.025 --> 00:02:42.085
Your task is only to accompany a few sensations.

21
00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:06.860
Keep enough attention on the path ahead to remain safe.

22
00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:16.000
Within that wider awareness, notice pressure changing across the soles: heel, middle, toes, or a pattern that does not fit those words.

23
00:03:37.024 --> 00:03:39.934
Feel how the knees and hips participate.

24
00:03:41.634 --> 00:03:45.474
The pelvis shifts, the arms may swing, and the spine responds.

25
00:03:47.174 --> 00:03:50.684
Walking is a whole-body movement, even when attention begins at the feet.

26
00:04:11.709 --> 00:04:14.639
If you reach the end of your path, pause before turning.

27
00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:17.569
Look where you are going.

28
00:04:19.269 --> 00:04:25.129
Turn in several ordinary steps, regain a steady direction, and continue only when the way is clear.

29
00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:45.200
Notice sounds and changes in light without chasing them.

30
00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:49.630
Let the walking body remain part of a real place.

31
00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:55.170
Awareness can include the environment and sensation together.

32
00:05:16.195 --> 00:05:19.715
Perhaps the mind has moved into plans, memories, or commentary.

33
00:05:21.415 --> 00:05:24.725
When you notice, use the next clear footfall as a place to return.

34
00:05:26.425 --> 00:05:29.305
No criticism is needed; returning is the practice.

35
00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:53.550
For several steps, feel the moment just before the foot lands.

36
00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:57.870
Then feel contact spread as weight is received.

37
00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:01.590
Let the ground be firm enough to meet each step.

38
00:06:26.119 --> 00:06:31.019
Now include the space between steps: the brief moment when weight is moving and balance is alive.

39
00:06:32.719 --> 00:06:35.759
There is no need to hold the body still inside movement.

40
00:06:56.784 --> 00:06:59.184
Allow your pace to become a little more natural.

41
00:07:00.884 --> 00:07:05.994
See whether attention can stay present without making the walk stiff or mechanical.

42
00:07:07.694 --> 00:07:10.914
Fluid movement and careful awareness can belong together.

43
00:07:35.443 --> 00:07:36.903
Notice effort.

44
00:07:38.603 --> 00:07:40.503
Some muscles work and others release.

45
00:07:42.203 --> 00:07:45.903
If the body is strained, shorten the path, slow down, or stop.

46
00:07:47.603 --> 00:07:52.303
Comfort is not required, but safety and workable effort matter.

47
00:08:13.328 --> 00:08:16.808
Choose three steps to know clearly from beginning to end.

48
00:08:18.508 --> 00:08:19.528
One step.

49
00:08:21.228 --> 00:08:21.938
Then another.

50
00:08:23.638 --> 00:08:24.928
Then one more.

51
00:08:26.628 --> 00:08:31.348
Afterward, let counting go and continue with a broad, uncomplicated awareness.

52
00:08:55.877 --> 00:09:00.307
Begin to finish by returning to your starting place or another safe stopping point.

53
00:09:02.007 --> 00:09:03.667
Let the pace settle.

54
00:09:05.367 --> 00:09:08.657
Stand still only after both feet have found reliable contact.

55
00:09:22.673 --> 00:09:29.473
Feel the after-sensation of walking: warmth, pulsing, steadiness, movement still echoing, or nothing distinct.

56
00:09:31.173 --> 00:09:32.983
Notice one breath while standing.

57
00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:49.750
Look around fully before you move on.

58
00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:54.090
This practice did not require leaving ordinary life.

59
00:09:55.790 --> 00:10:00.000
You practiced arriving inside an ordinary action, one safe step at a time.
